VALUES (
  '2026/11/06/hdq-thread-nov-06-2026',
  'thread',
  'thread',
  'Canada Lost 4,700 Jobs as America Added 165,000, and the Two Central Banks Now Face Opposite Labour Markets',
  'October employment diverged across the border: Canadian unemployment rose to 6.7% while the U.S. rate fell to 4.1%. The split sharpens the December rate dilemma after Thursday’s AI selloff.',
  '<ul>
<li><strong>Canada shed 4,700 jobs in October and unemployment rose to 6.7%.</strong><span> The employment rate slipped to 60.5%, its third consecutive monthly decline.</span></li>
<li><strong>The United States added 165,000 payroll jobs against a Reuters consensus of 65,000.</strong><span> Its unemployment rate eased to 4.1% and previous months were revised up by 84,000.</span></li>
<li><strong>Canadian private-sector payrolls fell by 53,000 while public-sector employment rose by 50,000.</strong><span> The headline near-zero change concealed a large change in composition.</span></li>
<li><strong>Canada’s youth unemployment rate reached 13.7%, versus 7.3% for Ontario overall.</strong><span> Long-term unemployment also rose as a share of the unemployed.</span></li>
<li><strong>The Fed and Bank of Canada enter December with different labour evidence.</strong><span> The Fed sees resilience, while Canada’s central bank must balance job weakness against energy inflation.</span></li>
</ul>',
  '<p>At 8:30 Friday morning, two labour reports delivered the clearest economic divergence of the week. Canada lost 4,700 jobs in October and its unemployment rate rose from 6.5% to 6.7%. The United States added 165,000 payroll jobs, more than twice the 65,000 Reuters consensus, and its unemployment rate slipped from 4.2% to 4.1%. Both economies face the inflationary consequences of the Iran war, but their labour markets are not giving their central banks the same permission to tighten.</p><p>The contrast matters after Thursday’s technology rout. The Nasdaq lost 5.52% and the S&amp;P 500 lost 4.36%, according to the previous HDQ closing thread. A fall in asset prices can tighten financial conditions without a rate increase. Friday’s labour numbers argue against treating that same market shock as equivalent evidence of economic weakness on both sides of the border.</p><h2>The Canadian Headline Conceals a Private-Sector Contraction</h2><p>Statistics Canada’s October survey shows employment little changed, but private-sector employees fell by 53,000 while public-sector employment rose by 50,000. The public-sector increase followed four consecutive monthly declines totalling 148,000. A flat national total therefore disguises a meaningful split between employers. Retail and wholesale trade shed 29,000 positions, while construction added 16,000 and educational services added 15,000.</p><p>The employment rate declined to 60.5%, its third straight monthly drop. Only 18.9% of people unemployed in September found work in October, compared with 24.6% on average in comparable months before the pandemic. Almost one-quarter of unemployed Canadians, 23.7%, had been looking for work for at least 27 weeks. Among workers aged 55 and older, that share reached 32.7%. These are measures of difficulty returning to employment, not simply a noisy monthly payroll estimate.</p><p>Youth unemployment climbed to 13.7%, a 0.7-point increase. Ontario’s rate rose to 7.3%, while Alberta’s jumped 0.9 points to 7.3%. The national figure therefore understates regional and generational exposure. In the same survey, 29.7% of Canadians lived in households reporting difficulty meeting financial needs, up from 27.7% a year earlier. That household stress is a direct consideration for advisers reviewing emergency reserves and withdrawal plans.</p><h2>The U.S. Report Makes a December Fed Hike Easier to Defend</h2><p>The U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics reported 165,000 new nonfarm payroll jobs in October, compared with an average monthly gain of 48,000 over the preceding year. Professional and business services accounted for 70,000 jobs, health care for 42,000 and transportation and warehousing for 25,000. Manufacturing added 19,000. August and September payrolls were revised upward by a combined 84,000.</p><p>Average hourly earnings increased 0.4% on the month and 3.1% over the year. The unemployment rate stood at 4.1%. That is stronger hiring evidence for a Federal Reserve already balancing energy inflation against a technology-market correction. It does not eliminate vulnerability: the number working part time for economic reasons rose by 339,000 to 4.8 million, showing that the headline strength is not universal.</p><div class="hdq-chart"><div style="background:#ffffff;border:1px solid #d0d0d0;width:100%;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;"><div style="background:#f5f5f5;border-bottom:1px solid #d0d0d0;padding:10px 14px;display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:16px;flex-wrap:wrap;"><span style="font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:#111;">OCTOBER LABOUR MARKET: CANADA VS UNITED STATES</span><span style="font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-left:auto;">OCT 2026 | OFFICIAL RELEASES NOV 6</span></div><div style="padding:12px 14px 8px;"><script>(function(){var _cs=document.currentScript,NS="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg",s=document.createElementNS(NS,"svg");s.setAttribute("viewBox","0 0 680 300");s.setAttribute("width","100%");function el(t,a,v){var e=document.createElementNS(NS,t);Object.keys(a).forEach(function(k){e.setAttribute(k,a[k])});if(v!==undefined)e.textContent=v;return e}var f="-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif";var d=[{name:"CANADA",value:-4.7,color:"#8a3030",x:195},{name:"UNITED STATES",value:165,color:"#4a5568",x:440}],zero=235,scale=0.85;s.appendChild(el("line",{x1:70,y1:zero,x2:620,y2:zero,stroke:"#999","stroke-width":1}));d.forEach(function(p){var h=Math.abs(p.value)*scale,y=p.value>=0?zero-h:zero;s.appendChild(el("rect",{x:p.x,y:y,width:92,height:h,fill:p.color}));s.appendChild(el("text",{x:p.x+46,y:p.value>=0?y-10:zero+h+18,"text-anchor":"middle","font-size":17,"font-weight":700,fill:"#111","font-family":f},(p.value>0?"+":"")+p.value.toFixed(1)+"k"));s.appendChild(el("text",{x:p.x+46,y:280,"text-anchor":"middle","font-size":11,fill:"#555","font-family":f},p.name))});s.appendChild(el("text",{x:70,y:20,"font-size":11,fill:"#777","font-family":f},"MONTHLY EMPLOYMENT CHANGE, THOUSANDS"));_cs.parentNode.appendChild(s)})();</script></div><div style="font-size:10px;color:#999;padding:4px 14px 10px;font-style:italic;">Source: Statistics Canada, Labour Force Survey, October 2026; U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, Employment Situation, October 2026, both released Nov. 6. &nbsp;|&nbsp; hdq.ca</div></div></div><p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">Monthly employment change, thousands of people. Canadian data come from the Labour Force Survey household measure; U.S. figures are nonfarm establishment payrolls. Different methodologies and population sizes mean the bars show direction, not comparable labour-market strength per capita.</p><h2>One Continent, Two December Decisions</h2><p>The Bank of Canada raised its overnight rate to 2.5% on Oct. 28 because it expected higher energy costs to spread beyond gasoline. Friday’s data make the trade-off sharper: employment has fallen in August, September and October, while the unemployment rate has risen for two consecutive months. A further increase on Dec. 9 would have to be defended against evidence of a softening labour market, not merely against a forecast of persistent inflation.</p><p>The Federal Reserve faces the reverse pressure. Its October meeting left rates at 3.75% to 4.00%, but Friday’s U.S. jobs report gives officials fresh evidence that demand can withstand restrictive policy. Reuters reported an immediate rise in Treasury yields after the release. The subsequent equity rebound does not erase Thursday’s loss or settle the AI investment question.</p><p>For Canadian advisers the useful distinction is not which country had the better headline. It is that Canadian borrowing costs, U.S. equity valuations and the Canadian dollar may respond to different rate paths. Review variable-rate debt against another 25-basis-point increase, test the currency effect on unhedged U.S. assets, and avoid turning a single Friday rebound into a new long-term allocation. The next decisions require two separate labour narratives.</p>',
